index 0000000..4e7bf58
--- /dev/null
+++ b/HISTORY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# Historical notes for the new seas
+
+These are **arcade interpretations**, not documentary recreations. Distances,
+ship speeds, hit points, numbers of ships and repeatable wave battles are scaled
+for play. The music is newly composed in period-inspired textures, **not** a
+recording or reconstruction of a historical tune.
+
+- **First Barbary War (1801–1805).** The player commands the American frigate
+  *USS Constitution* against Tripolitan xebecs and corsairs. Early US Navy
+  ships sailed and fought off Tripoli. [Naval History and Heritage Command:
+  USS Constitution](https://www.history.navy.mil/content/history/nhhc/browse-by-topic/ships/original-frigates/uss-constitution-americas-ship-of-state/background-for-media/brief-history.html)
+- **Battle of Jutland (1916).** *HMS Lion* faces German torpedo boats and
+  the battlecruiser *SMS Lützow*. Shells and surface torpedoes replace the
+  square-rigged fleets. [Imperial War Museums: Jutland and HMS Lion](https://www.iwm.org.uk/history/first-world-war/jutland/major-francis-harvey-vc)
+- **Guadalcanal (1942).** *USS Fletcher* uses naval guns and limited,
+  unguided surface torpedoes against destroyers and supply traffic.
+  [Naval History and Heritage Command: Fletcher (DD-445)](https://www.history.navy.mil/research/histories/ship-histories/danfs/f/fletcher.html)
+- **Operation Praying Mantis (1988).** *USS Simpson* uses missiles and deck
+  guns against Iranian fast craft, including the missile boat *Joshan*. Oil
+  tankers are protected neutral traffic, **not targets to plunder**. This
+  follows the tanker escort and US–Iran naval confrontations in and around the
+  Persian Gulf and Strait of Hormuz in 1987–88; the game does not suggest that
+  this was the first interaction of the two countries. [Naval History and
+  Heritage Command: Simpson (FFG-56)](https://www.history.navy.mil/research/histories/ship-histories/danfs/s/simpson--ffg-56--ii-1985-.html)
+
+Earlier, **pre-gunpowder** scenarios equip ship crews with arrows, winch-spanned
+bolts and (for the Byzantine dromon) Greek fire rather than round shot. A
+Spanish ship appearing in a first-contact scenario keeps its period guns. Some
+inhabited shores form named compacts: attacking a village rallies her allies;
+independent shores remain neutral.
